46-- Use the test site URL, usernames, and passwords documented in the QA materials for the active stage or the credential source referenced there. Do not rely on memory or stale hardcoded credentials from another file.
47:- Always use Vercel's agent-browser CLI first for any browser automation or browser navigation. Before running commands, load its usage guide with `agent-browser skills get core` (add `--full` for the complete command reference); for exploratory testing or bug hunts also load `agent-browser skills get dogfood`.
48:- Drive pages with the snapshot-and-ref loop: `agent-browser open <url>`, then `agent-browser snapshot -i` to see interactive elements, then act on the `@eN` refs (`click`, `fill`, `type`, `select`). Re-snapshot after any navigation or DOM change. Keep auth states separate with isolated `--session <role>` sessions (e.g. admin/customer/guest), and capture screenshots (`agent-browser screenshot --full`) to understand the UI before deciding whether behavior passes or fails.
49:- When QA requires WordPress CLI commands, always run them through WP-CLI with `--allow-root` in this environment. Do not set extra environment variables for QA WP-related commands unless a stage explicitly requires it; debug logging and the necessary QA setup are already in place.
50-- QA must be real end-to-end browser testing against the actual test site. Do not replace it with API-only checks, mocked flows, or code-only reasoning when browser verification is required.
51-- Capture screenshots and concrete observations from the browser session whenever they help verify behavior or explain a failure. Use screenshots to inspect and understand the UI before judging layout, visibility, or interaction issues.
52-- Always update both QA kanban boards during QA work. This is mandatory and must not be skipped.
53-- Log current QA progress in `qa/progress/`.
54-- In `qa/progress/`, add the individual tasks from the current stage, not the stage name itself.
55-- Name progress tasks with the stage-prefixed task format, for example `stage-0: Task Name 1` and `stage-0: Task Name 2`.
56-- Log bugs, regressions, blockers, and other QA issues in `qa/issues/`.
57-- When creating any new QA issue in `qa/issues/`, include these fields in the issue body before moving on:
58-  - QA progress task ID and stage, plus the relevant QA plan markdown file path.
59-  - Affected subscription ID(s) and order ID(s), or `N/A` if the issue has none.
60:  - Affected WordPress user/customer ID(s), login/email, and role(s), or `N/A` if the issue has none.
61:  - Exact test URL/admin route, browser/user context, reproduction steps, expected result, actual result, and concrete proof such as UI text, WP-CLI/REST output, DB/meta values, or screenshot observations.
62-  - Known scope notes or counterexamples, especially when a similar flow works on a different subscription, order, user, gateway, or setting.
63-- Before running any `kanban-md` command for QA tracking, `cd` into the target board directory first:
64-  `qa/progress/` for progress tasks and `qa/issues/` for bug tasks.
65-  Do not run QA kanban commands from the plugin root, because that can create a new board in the wrong working directory.
66-- Do not install Composer dependencies for QA or routine validation in this workspace. Use the pre-built environment and pre-built assets already present.
