78:## agent-browser Usage
79-
80:Vercel's agent-browser is the single tool for browser automation, QA verification, and any browser navigation. It is a native CLI driving Chrome/Chromium over CDP and manages its own browser, so no manual Chrome launch or sandbox flags are needed.
81-
82-Setup:
83-
84:- Install once: `npm i -g agent-browser && agent-browser install`.
85:- Load the version-matched guide before using it: `agent-browser skills get core` (overview + common patterns) or `agent-browser skills get core --full` (full command reference). For exploratory testing and bug hunts load `agent-browser skills get dogfood`; run `agent-browser skills list` for the rest.
86-
87-Core loop:
88-
89-```bash
90:agent-browser open https://mirror-help.arrayhash.com/wp-admin   # navigate
91:agent-browser snapshot -i                                       # interactive elements + @eN refs
92:agent-browser click @e3                                         # act on a ref
93:agent-browser snapshot -i                                       # re-snapshot after any change
94:agent-browser screenshot --full /tmp/state.png                 # capture to judge the UI
95-```
96-
97-Rules:
98-
99-- Refs (`@e1`, `@e2`, …) are reassigned on every snapshot and go stale after navigation, form submits, or re-renders — always re-snapshot before the next ref interaction.
100:- Use isolated sessions for different auth contexts: `agent-browser --session admin …`, `--session customer …`, `--session guest …`. Persist or restore login with `--session-name`, `state save`/`state load`, or the auth vault as needed.
101:- Prefer precise waits over fixed sleeps: `wait @ref`, `wait --text "…"`, `wait --url "**/wp-admin"`, `wait --load networkidle`.
102-- Capture before/after/error screenshots and base UI judgments on those plus snapshot, DOM, and network observations (`network requests`, `console`, `errors`).
103:- Close sessions when done: `agent-browser close --all`.
104-
105-## Non-Negotiable Rules
106-
107-### Plugin Ownership
108-
109-- Put shared subscription engine behavior, free modules, shared lifecycle rules, shared support classes, and shared contracts in `arraysubs`.
110-- Put premium-only modules, premium UI, premium automations, premium integrations, and premium workflows in `arraysubspro`.
111-- If a feature spans both plugins:
112-  1. Add the reusable contract or extension point in `arraysubs`.
113-  2. Add the premium implementation in `arraysubspro`.
114-  3. Verify core still works correctly without pro active.
115-
116-### Cross-Plugin Alignment
117-
118-When changing shared behavior, review both plugins for:
119-
120-- hooks and filters
121-- helper functions and service responsibilities
122-- REST routes, schemas, and payload shapes
123-- post meta, options, transients, status names, and table names
124-- Action Scheduler hooks, groups, and arguments
125:- asset handles, admin routes, and runtime data objects
126-- subscription lifecycle, billing, renewal, grace-period, and cancellation flows
127-
128-Prefer backward-compatible changes. Do not silently break `arraysubspro` through core refactors.
129-
130-### Shared Support And Config
131-
132-- Reuse shared support classes from `ArraySubs\Supports\...`.
133-- Use the core `ArraySubs\Supports\Config` store.
134-- Shared runtime values stay under existing core scopes such as `plugin.*`, `wp.*`, and `settings.*`.
135-- Add only pro-specific runtime values under `proplugin.*`.
136-- Do not create a second shared support layer in `arraysubspro`.
137-
138-### Subscription Data Standard
139-
140-- Treat subscriptions as `WP_Post` objects, not arrays.
141-- Use `arraysubs_get_subscription()` to retrieve subscriptions.
142-- Read subscription meta with `get_post_meta($subscription->ID, ...)`.
143-- When firing subscription-related hooks, pass a `WP_Post` object as the subscription payload.
144-- Handlers should stay defensive for backward compatibility, but new code must use `WP_Post`.
145-
146-### Action Scheduler Standard
147-
148-- Use the centralized `ArraySubs\Supports\ActionScheduler` class.
149-- Use defined `HOOK_*` and `GROUP_*` constants, never raw strings.
150-- Pass indexed arrays for scheduler args, not associative arrays.
151-- Use scheduler locks in handlers to prevent duplicate concurrent work.
152-- Keep new scheduled actions aligned with the existing registration and unscheduling patterns.
153-- Renewal scheduling uses both `HOOK_GENERATE_RENEWAL_INVOICE` for precise per-subscription invoice generation and `HOOK_PROCESS_RENEWAL` for due-time payment processing; keep both actions aligned when changing renewal dates.

